  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.run.unreadable",
        "system.regular-file",
        "system.platforms"
      ],
      "text": "lint.run.unreadable sorts entries into [symbolic links], [regular file]s and folders, but 'folder' is not defined. system.regular-file now counts a Windows reparse point that is no name surrogate as a regular file, but nothing says whether a folder carrying such a reparse point (such as a OneDrive Files On-Demand folder) is a folder or one of the 'other entries' lint refuses. That is a difference between platforms that system.platforms requires the tree to decide.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in da17030"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "system.regular-file",
        "system.symbolic-link"
      ],
      "text": "The two definitions do not settle every Windows reparse point that is no name surrogate. 'Holds a file's data itself' and 'includes a reparse point that is no name surrogate' disagree on one that holds no data (such as an AF_UNIX socket). One that stands for another entry by name without being a name surrogate (such as an app execution alias) fits both terms, because the Windows sentence of system.symbolic-link does not say whether its list is complete.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in da17030"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "baseline.manifest.remove",
        "system.symbolic-link"
      ],
      "text": "baseline.manifest.remove says a 'link' named `.coherence.json` or `.amendment.md` is removed 'without what it points to', but uses plain prose instead of the new [symbolic link] term. So it is not decided whether a junction or mount point standing there counts as a link, removed alone, or as a folder, removed with everything in it, which could delete content outside the spec root.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in da17030"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "baseline.manifest.remove"
      ],
      "text": "The Then says how each entry is removed (a folder with everything in it, a [symbolic link] without what it points to), but the Otherwise says only that each of <entries> that can be removed is removed. So when one entry fails and another is a symbolic link or junction, no rule says that the link's target stays, or that a folder goes with everything in it.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in b76e2f0"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "baseline.manifest.remove",
        "baseline.run.writes"
      ],
      "text": "Only a symbolic link that is itself one of <entries> is said to go without what it points to. A symbolic link or junction inside a folder named `.coherence.json` or `.amendment.md` falls under 'a folder with everything in it', and no rule or row says that its target, which may be in the spec tree or outside it, stays. `baseline.run.writes` allows those names to change 'each with everything in it' and does not settle it either.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in b76e2f0"
    },
    {
      "id": "f6",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "baseline.manifest.remove"
      ],
      "text": "No rule or row says what happens to the contents of a folder entry (`.coherence.json` or `.amendment.md`) that cannot be removed as a whole because only part of what it holds can be removed: Otherwise removes only 'each of <entries> that can be removed', so whether the removable files and links inside such a folder are removed or left is undecided.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "Pre-existing, and harmless: whatever part of the entry stays is left over, which verify reports, as the rule's Rationale says; the partial state decides nothing."
    }
  ]
